[{:db/id "q-36947/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f9fd98e0-fff7-59dd-a173-80eaa71b0617" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36947/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "c103ac9f-569e-596f-bb9f-b41588904e2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36947/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "ece4dddc-2ea6-50d1-a330-af5b80f2a82e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36947/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "9031ad5c-ecde-50be-97fd-0d64f8b23ad3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36947/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "8a269e8d-5b32-5129-901a-9e2394669d7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36947/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2accb363-3ea6-5fb0-8f2c-41b34a63d9d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36947/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer-field/choices ["q-36947/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-36947/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-36947/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-36947/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-36947/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36947" :question/id #uuid "851cfff5-395d-5456-8bc7-458fa6f08e6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36947" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -9 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-9){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & -27 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=(-\\frac{1}{9}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd one). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-2{x}_{3}.$ From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-3{x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{-3}{1}=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-36947/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36947"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-36814"] :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =7,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that its augmented matrix $M$ is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=9,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "683fa59d-ca01-5887-8ea4-d8edb1b97207" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d4212a08-7dc9-504f-a39d-65b9efe1f20d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f3bca541-ffa2-546d-bb43-ab8450bb9955" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "0aa1afd8-15e3-57b6-8a65-e6f04fc0a721" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "2655f0b7-0ac1-5fb0-b4bb-7f69895b00f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3153942a-88df-560b-93c6-1ff857a89306" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37399/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer-field/choices ["q-37399/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-37399/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-37399/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-37399/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-37399/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37399" :question/id #uuid "c9a5bc14-9aaf-5e50-ac9e-ba8b81836ace" :question/math-academy-id "q-37399" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}21 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -12 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -7\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 20{x}_{1}-13{x}_{2}-9{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 18{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 18 & -12 & -8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{9}{10}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}20 & -13 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{10} & \\frac{1}{10} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{20}(13{x}_{2}+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}(13(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+9{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37399/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37399"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "1e0d499d-078d-5adf-999a-db089930ac2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "729f2135-4be1-5b22-b034-c29628a3ec72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "03a12a50-3695-5ffb-be4c-2832fe51fee6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "f9a3209a-61a2-5d65-8383-33a9f78fc296" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "9b569aec-0ad8-5332-8e40-be567891dccb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "d78e5724-04c3-51a5-b9b2-7962b1a6dbf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36752/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-36752/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-36752/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-36752/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-36752/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-36752/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36752" :question/id #uuid "ff9da2e7-ba10-5f61-b9e6-9c9796cb2f7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36752" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-7{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 10{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -8 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}8 & -7 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{8}(7{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(7(\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=3,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-36752/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36752"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "0a0cd18b-5d31-556f-93ec-67de350d7e13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "6bf57869-e9c6-501a-806b-c1f83e798007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "96ac7dd4-fb63-557f-9798-048e3bb37347" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "f5ea9efa-fc1a-54f7-a618-a09263cecda8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "0ac8bb23-45bd-5030-895c-3f89a1e7898a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "19d8b1e0-f256-5b0d-9bd5-f4574d42cfdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37379/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-37379/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-37379/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-37379/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-37379/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-37379/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37379" :question/id #uuid "ccb0542b-3ce5-5bc8-82b8-99e0d02b16f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-37379" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vector $v$ and the matrix $A$ are given above. \nIf $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -8 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 8\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-10{x}_{2}+6{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 5 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=3{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-5{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(-5(3{x}_{3})+7{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ 3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{1}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37379/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2c79885-7848-51e3-9f96-6a109e776fd5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37379"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419/selection/9a0415857e758bb9726a9776b6f441064bbcf3f836adf33eade3692260f5b654" :answer/id #uuid "a9c2f0d4-d36c-5368-a963-bb75d34e33bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419/selection/bca31af5f53a2f4ebdfb6c1b6e2da61ff7ed14105224f91b03471739142ce257" :answer/id #uuid "31d78fc4-3c30-5eef-89da-2bdd749920b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419/selection/14cdbb78badfa6613fdb4a6e7df1cbafd517c9d7463dc360907eabfab789630b" :answer/id #uuid "db13efdc-4937-54a0-b367-0747d4055a5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419/selection/ea3e6846ce88edc5788cda469cb379e7858b0005a50008ec71ef9d8d18531d6e" :answer/id #uuid "76870c09-96be-5b0f-ac8c-dc372736434f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419/selection/e11f2c9f4980989f658632b796dff11bc402d2275b0f8fbb91273a7319217e09" :answer/id #uuid "daa92a92-9a4f-5e04-8aa4-4b9561a7c92d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "19d39da7-dc72-5459-a5ed-520ae68960af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-89419/selection/e11f2c9f4980989f658632b796dff11bc402d2275b0f8fbb91273a7319217e09" :answer-field/choices ["q-89419/selection/9a0415857e758bb9726a9776b6f441064bbcf3f836adf33eade3692260f5b654" "q-89419/selection/bca31af5f53a2f4ebdfb6c1b6e2da61ff7ed14105224f91b03471739142ce257" "q-89419/selection/14cdbb78badfa6613fdb4a6e7df1cbafd517c9d7463dc360907eabfab789630b" "q-89419/selection/ea3e6846ce88edc5788cda469cb379e7858b0005a50008ec71ef9d8d18531d6e" "q-89419/selection/e11f2c9f4980989f658632b796dff11bc402d2275b0f8fbb91273a7319217e09"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-89419" :question/id #uuid "0e67636c-732e-5418-86ed-bc13bbb0d106" :question/math-academy-id "q-89419" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-14 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 17 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+12{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -20{x}_{1}+15{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -20 & 15 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{5}{4}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 12 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=4,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-89419/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89419"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/selection/2a2dfa122b83e1fbc6b70be6ef717447914d5f79f3dfea05d917ce624c516d65" :answer/id #uuid "d43d2da4-e505-5ed6-b0f0-6122f87d3148" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/selection/097bf54e53fd44fd496a8806d7ef0ea4f567d8a5a994b298b0fe8b19f4b98622" :answer/id #uuid "87c86e42-3f3f-5878-baf1-ee3d0eb1193d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/selection/a51b5d98d135312d006e556f37cffdda39fee6cebfacdc46fdfba370ab35568c" :answer/id #uuid "330f7b61-b744-54a1-9a1e-0dab732efc20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/selection/9d1e17d9784da5ae0067ade351923f4b0836785a26b2360fd2d3ae75fb99bcb4" :answer/id #uuid "be1d675f-2327-58ab-812c-4e8b190e62bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/selection/f9ba8df6b0717280f10c420a8809aaf270540eeb781f0c9993e96a763aaec3a3" :answer/id #uuid "ac2f0f5c-07ad-582b-abd8-4d1a30d45cdf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf091ce-24c0-5ce6-9af2-69f4d0310482" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83481/selection/a51b5d98d135312d006e556f37cffdda39fee6cebfacdc46fdfba370ab35568c" :answer-field/choices ["q-83481/selection/2a2dfa122b83e1fbc6b70be6ef717447914d5f79f3dfea05d917ce624c516d65" "q-83481/selection/097bf54e53fd44fd496a8806d7ef0ea4f567d8a5a994b298b0fe8b19f4b98622" "q-83481/selection/a51b5d98d135312d006e556f37cffdda39fee6cebfacdc46fdfba370ab35568c" "q-83481/selection/9d1e17d9784da5ae0067ade351923f4b0836785a26b2360fd2d3ae75fb99bcb4" "q-83481/selection/f9ba8df6b0717280f10c420a8809aaf270540eeb781f0c9993e96a763aaec3a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83481" :question/id #uuid "a11ee4d1-3e88-5212-80d0-293758418f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-83481" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 13 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-11{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -14{x}_{1}+11{x}_{2}-8{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 9 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -14 & 11 & -8 & 0 \\\\ -11 & 9 & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{11}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{7}{2} & -7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{7}{8}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=2{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83481/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83481"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719/selection/254905d59d5c192c0a7767415d322faea669bab94a509d6367bfaf67b813564a" :answer/id #uuid "b6ac3cb2-ec7d-5901-9ea3-f9be58c203f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719/selection/da1ad5875e26b3d0c918e4de924a596bce15b8ac51cee7ac7659358732671ec4" :answer/id #uuid "1bde35b9-6f6c-5f9f-8d72-534493c1b9d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719/selection/6ad1bed2f2881f536d97fde8c96c81e651712ccb3fd807d096f18bda7715867e" :answer/id #uuid "a7d68e94-fd7e-5dcb-aed8-923ba48066c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719/selection/e68d9bac1a0ee742552e7aa472bd1cdbf9089e57f2f690099a9f9f267958d11b" :answer/id #uuid "16bebea2-a29e-5690-be4c-46067700dbcf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719/selection/d3b1638f9f3576293a777a8962e6d764352e8ec3044eac45b730b13f747a5385" :answer/id #uuid "21bc30ba-1ba8-5a94-9a15-2eb94d10a8c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a10f32b-c6ad-5a87-addb-5992ae0ecc65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140719/selection/e68d9bac1a0ee742552e7aa472bd1cdbf9089e57f2f690099a9f9f267958d11b" :answer-field/choices ["q-140719/selection/254905d59d5c192c0a7767415d322faea669bab94a509d6367bfaf67b813564a" "q-140719/selection/da1ad5875e26b3d0c918e4de924a596bce15b8ac51cee7ac7659358732671ec4" "q-140719/selection/6ad1bed2f2881f536d97fde8c96c81e651712ccb3fd807d096f18bda7715867e" "q-140719/selection/e68d9bac1a0ee742552e7aa472bd1cdbf9089e57f2f690099a9f9f267958d11b" "q-140719/selection/d3b1638f9f3576293a777a8962e6d764352e8ec3044eac45b730b13f747a5385"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140719" :question/id #uuid "3918bdee-518b-5fd4-8072-467ee033f405" :question/math-academy-id "q-140719" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-{x}_{2}+2(0)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140719/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140719"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751/selection/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :answer/id #uuid "5fac810f-ca7e-515f-a91b-e5ef016d2b19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751/selection/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" :answer/id #uuid "1f6d2fdf-faa4-5c11-a5ca-1b06f8b3be3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" :answer/id #uuid "edea20f2-d8b3-5c4d-906f-c49259d31c56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751/selection/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388" :answer/id #uuid "a284baad-8ff5-5d01-9762-bbeb544d2401" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" :answer/id #uuid "45ecfd9f-3e99-57e2-9342-f8f1584825da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cfe22339-b061-507d-82bd-5852979d9aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36751/selection/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" :answer-field/choices ["q-36751/selection/82c79a11c5f316588a4977bf9de059017422d38604e630b6407357df3b622d75" "q-36751/selection/f98c18a27dcb118caf5a5576c91aab2f7c6ef2dcdc9bdea8485ada011f865eac" "q-36751/selection/a3a5487afcc81a1c06c56ceaee82744a099a79debc8be0e8469f77e68bf12a04" "q-36751/selection/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388" "q-36751/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36751" :question/id #uuid "d8eadfe0-8324-566b-8c56-2906c1bb6d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-36751" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $2$nd and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{1}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{1}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36751/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36751"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717/selection/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" :answer/id #uuid "b6922873-5910-536a-a3fa-9d861cd997fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717/selection/c04062beac8667663907226256b811560f51da810319e759ed7238d97bbb0329" :answer/id #uuid "9275f56b-36c7-5f68-bf64-cb231b70c1d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717/selection/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388" :answer/id #uuid "c2e439e9-b1d6-5da0-b398-74228fa8397a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" :answer/id #uuid "90a61817-5aa6-50ba-a87a-528b6d7cf506" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717/selection/eb3cd9e99842edb1208bec99aa7d747cb8011c78ace0517019b8154ca2a087fd" :answer/id #uuid "91417102-b5af-545d-af6f-0bb6ce78ff5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "609077e4-1a9e-52a9-b782-c8ff5cb3c4cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140717/selection/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388" :answer-field/choices ["q-140717/selection/074db705e16e1e57f8a2aff94b5fe57d6f39b7233e5e76f422f3f665da482360" "q-140717/selection/c04062beac8667663907226256b811560f51da810319e759ed7238d97bbb0329" "q-140717/selection/d23151519d79f27e4cebc4ebad9047e36b8453244e6e3d202a56b90321183388" "q-140717/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" "q-140717/selection/eb3cd9e99842edb1208bec99aa7d747cb8011c78ace0517019b8154ca2a087fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140717" :question/id #uuid "69767f87-3126-5b96-9f71-4c31e6b8f43f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140717" :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=(\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & ={x}_{2}+2(0) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140717/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4def2d61-0093-53cf-bcbb-1d2c3e6e1738"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140717"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "876fd0b9-5c30-5541-996b-a3b86daaf0bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "aae02240-9b8c-5d18-a556-b3fdfddde046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "a84f1b8e-ba13-5f42-a0e3-2adc58848db4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "519086d0-8755-5541-aeca-fcc53bfb0da9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "a0633f05-3104-5259-9061-c3e09b017b18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cbe73ba-5aa0-5391-be0d-6fee1eb9d8e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37403/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-37403/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-37403/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-37403/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-37403/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-37403/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37403" :question/id #uuid "2e9a1415-54c8-5ab9-beb4-2dda4d267cb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37403" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(2)(2)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{20}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=4{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(-2(4{x}_{3})-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-4{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{-4}{1}=-4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37403/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37403"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "3f651c4a-ecc8-5c3c-98c1-4022fdc5f53d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "7ea8b7c1-ad8f-50f3-954e-488e365d5a13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "3bdb9142-b4a3-57e8-b43d-6a3561e82142" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" :answer/id #uuid "2a8630e4-41e6-5680-afba-5ba0cdce6f63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer/id #uuid "48f3030e-9a65-55a4-b237-e31fd20436c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddf8bdd-0a61-5a3d-8a6f-f29f8a795082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140720/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe" :answer-field/choices ["q-140720/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-140720/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" "q-140720/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-140720/selection/4de395131b8c6206950603752bf3404c42f4dd6f0a41fee38e42388d1b92c133" "q-140720/selection/7f2aea1619f9c4cbe5384e0826442d82ebdb1ac614452244ced54b63c0c77efe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140720" :question/id #uuid "91db9e2c-382d-5e25-9d74-2311bbfc692c" :question/math-academy-id "q-140720" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}+2{x}_{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{7}{1}=7.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140720/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140720"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "b82625de-591e-562c-aa0d-f0662fac097b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "759db6d3-bc1e-5ec8-b61a-4461d47529a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer/id #uuid "72dabe73-042b-5dcb-bf4d-1e2bd544d5dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "21c5b8bf-6781-57ae-b25d-3beddbf4a42c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "fa61dc56-1cac-5008-b469-8fe5b67f69d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "11eee310-ed78-5fbe-93ef-41616b28ac33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-89427/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" :answer-field/choices ["q-89427/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-89427/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-89427/selection/f80535409f78d0e1b5cd0ceb7e72783093e3cde8adf28a5032c83c6dc6b5e490" "q-89427/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-89427/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-89427" :question/id #uuid "8781a920-640d-5eec-863f-cde359137a07" :question/math-academy-id "q-89427" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-4-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-(-4)(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -24) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +6)(\\lambda -4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-6.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-6.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]-(-6)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+8{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 8 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ From the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=2{x}_{3}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-89427/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89427"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "d9d109c1-c769-5aac-b1a5-c1377741d0b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "aec4e42f-7d3c-5176-990a-ea5bd5c35744" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" :answer/id #uuid "e6ad8839-0cd8-5080-8971-37c66c1f37e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423/selection/b937b5a9c9148a5914243385a1d84bacb019d6c5ff771ce01182a0f3091b9285" :answer/id #uuid "f47e5117-c2c5-59ee-bcff-87919b321edd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "28d7b76a-9690-528a-a990-8227badf346e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "02516065-d4f5-5816-ae15-b7fd983b8424" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-89423/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-89423/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-89423/selection/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" "q-89423/selection/d278d4936eb1c69dc660a77f5f1c7b1eb5b4a4db4aee8a8ad2dc52aa3f827f2e" "q-89423/selection/b937b5a9c9148a5914243385a1d84bacb019d6c5ff771ce01182a0f3091b9285" "q-89423/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-89423" :question/id #uuid "d0c3bc9c-fd88-560a-8f9d-9416328a7ea4" :question/math-academy-id "q-89423" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5{x}_{3}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =2{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{8}{4}=2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-89423/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e732a2b-a6a1-5e0f-b43e-a9fbc02cfc74"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-89423"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37385"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[a,\\,b,\\,c]^{T}$ such that the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find all the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(-2)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ correspond to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Thus, the third eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ must correspond to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-1.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=0.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5490"] :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the matrix $A$ are given below. \nIf $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=3,$ then what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/5f1bfa33d135d95e3153e3305da50b69541d88d1cd78b5b6ad030a89861f3e10.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=x_3.$ From the first equation, we get $x_1=3x_3.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/f2707b01c13749362887e4ae0da130466ae753dccd4bc354600af79f71c3de5f.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/8e7627a6a515086ff53f5ed250a070e08ee2b71d0e4fb3d79e5434218d028624.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{b}{c}=\\dfrac{1}{1}=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5491"] :question/problem "Find an eigenvector of\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/33471f345fdb027b0440014631bf0dadff706ffb76938b67b15db66fd80dcae6.png)\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =-2.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $3$rd). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_3=0.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get $x_1=-x_2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/97b9d021ad0a9800e0b6a2b696c3908f2f5bec64fa496a1c2222ae5b86a7b56b.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_2=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/b489148b8e378e6168ddd9dbba18200f10f2788df69aa7100d20b341116102fd.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5492"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/1503285c91d0c320a3720c702b620bfe2156ed713d915b04c22bd35c06a05cd8.png)\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1$ of the matrix $A,$ given above. Find the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3=\\big[ a, \\, b, \\, c \\big]^T$ such that the set $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent. What is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a diagonal matrix, we have that its eigenvalues are the entries in the main diagonal, that is, $\\lambda_1=1,$ $\\lambda_2=-1$ and $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nAlso, since we obtained three distinct eigenvalues, the set of eigenvectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2, \\mathbf{v}_3 \\},$ where each vector corresponds to different eigenvalue, will be linearly independent.\n\nNow, recall that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ correspond to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=1$ and $\\lambda_2=-1.$ Thus, the third eigenvector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ must correspond to $\\lambda_3=4.$ \n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =4.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the augmented matrix $M$ is already in its row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this matrix, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the first and second equations, we obtain $x_1=x_2=0.$  Therefore, the general solution is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/7aab21fae627c5fe36b144f4a1e12a2d5e57ef7c2c987fbee64f718c7cbb7404.png)\n\nSetting, for instance, $x_3=1,$ we get the following eigenvector:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069621/assets/c080641a794b7a65f7893429d32920f39bfb4d87d9193197afd6b48dacc697a3.png)\n\nFinally, $\\dfrac{a}{c}=\\dfrac{0}{1}=0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
